  "notes": "London's one-man analog-soul auteur — Sault, Cleo Sol and Little Simz all come off the same board. The drum block is authored from a first-hand credit rather than a description: \"**he plays the drums himself** (credited across drums, bass, piano, synthesiser, guitar, vocals), which is the parameter: a dry, close, quiet, unquantised kit with the kick on 1 and 3, a soft short-decay snare on 2&4, minimal hats, heavy room bleed on the funk tracks and near-total damping on the ballads, plus a dense hand-percussion layer. **Nothing is gated or replaced.**\" So `quantizeStrength` is 0.46 with a real snare displacement, `snare.layered` is false (nothing replaced), the hat fill density is 0.1, and the percussion runs 5–9 hits a bar across five lanes at −2 dB — the densest hand-percussion set of the producer models here. Harmony is \"hymn-and-70s-soul written on piano — 4-to-8-bar cycles with real cadences and modulation on the song-form material, against one-bar modal m7 vamps on the Afro-funk material. **He never uses a 2-chord pop loop as a default.**\" That last sentence is why `maxChords` stays at 4 and the harmonic rhythm moves every bar rather than vamping, unlike almost every other modern model in this section. Bass is \"played electric bass, dry and mixed forward, often a single repeating one-bar figure that is the track's entire harmonic content\", which is an independent riff at a low root probability with fixed cell shapes — and no `bass808` block, because the entry says \"no 808 anywhere\". He is described as an **arranger** who \"assigns each section a different ensemble\", and the release practice is \"album-as-suite with no gaps, very short tracks, abrupt cuts\" — the short sections and the high `switchUpProb`. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are every drum probability and velocity and all melody figures; the research sources the instruments, credits and album practice but marks the drum values as inferred.",
